internal/silk transcribes the SILK constant tables of RFC 6716 section 4.2
from the same reference implementation, under the same licence. The
transcription is performed by internal/testutil/silktables and checked back
against the reference by the package's conformance test.

internal/silk/testdata/gen_silk.c links against that reference to generate
the vectors the decoder is checked against.

RFC 6716 names this coder and describes its role but does not state the
procedure, so the reference implementation is its only normative statement.

mp3 recovers its constant tables from minimp3, by Lion (lieff), released into
the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal. CC0 asks for no attribution; this
records the derivation because the project's own policy is that every source is
named.

MP3 has no freely readable specification — ISO/IEC 11172-3 and 13818-3 are sold,
and MPEG-2.5 was never standardised at all — and minimp3 is the only
permissively licensed implementation there is. What was taken is the standard's
data rather than minimp3's code:

  tables.go   the thirty-two Huffman tables, the two quadruple tables, the
              scale factor band layouts and the scale factor sizing tables.
              Recovered by internal/testutil/mp3tables, which walks minimp3's
              packed lookups over every bit pattern they can consume and records
              what comes out, then writes the codes as a binary tree. Checked
              before it is written: every table must be a complete prefix code
              and the tree must reproduce its own codes.

  window.go   the five-hundred-and-twelve-tap synthesis window, which minimp3
              does not hold either — it keeps it folded into an arrangement of
              its own. Recovered by measurement: one impulse in one subband
              gives that subband's response, and the response is the window
              modulated by a known cosine. Thirty-two subbands give thirty-two
              readings of each tap, agreeing to two parts in a million.

  mp3/testdata  the oracles the decoder is checked against, produced by running
              a traced minimp3 over the compliance bitstreams it distributes.
              Measurements of its behaviour, not its source.

  Copyright: none claimed. CC0 1.0 Universal, see
  https://github.com/lieff/minimp3.

